# Copy to `.env`. It is loaded by `src/lib/server/env.ts`, which the server, the
# scripts and drizzle-kit all import — so one file configures all of them. A real
# environment variable always wins, so a container ignores it.

# The SQLite file. One per instance; the directory is created on first run.
# Mount this path as a volume when running the container — it is the database.
DATABASE_PATH=.data/kilorep.db

# Where the generated SQL migrations live. Only set this if the folder moves;
# the server applies them at boot and the container must ship them.
# MIGRATIONS_DIR=drizzle

# Whether a Google identity nobody has seen before becomes a new account. Off
# unless set to a plain yes (`1`, `true`, `yes`, `on`); anything else, including
# a typo, leaves it shut. You do not need this to get started — `bun run
# account:create` makes the first account locally, without exposing anything.
# Turn it on only for an instance that genuinely serves more than one person.
#
# It does not gate *linking*: a Google identity whose verified address already
# belongs to an account attaches to it either way, which is how you move your own
# `account:create` account onto Google without opening the instance to strangers.
# ALLOW_REGISTRATION=false

# Google sign-in. Unset on both is the ordinary case and not a misconfiguration:
# the sign-in screen simply does not offer it, and accounts made with
# `account:create` sign in with their password as before. Set both to turn it on.
#
# From console.cloud.google.com → APIs & Services → Credentials → OAuth client ID
# → Web application. Two things have to match or nothing works:
#
#   Authorized redirect URI   exactly `<your origin>/api/auth/google/callback`,
#                             e.g. https://kilorep.example.com/api/auth/google/callback.
#                             A mismatch fails on Google's own page, as
#                             `redirect_uri_mismatch`, before this server sees it.
#                             Behind a proxy, PROTOCOL_HEADER below is what stops
#                             the server deriving `http://` and asking for the
#                             wrong one.
#   Publishing status         "Testing" limits sign-in to the test users you list
#                             in the console — nobody else can sign in at all.
#                             "In production" needs an app homepage and a privacy
#                             policy URL on the consent screen. The scopes used
#                             here (openid, email, profile) are non-sensitive, so
#                             no Google review is involved either way.
#
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET is a real secret. Keep it out of version control and out
# of anything world-readable — on NixOS that means an `environmentFile`, never a
# value written into the module, because the Nix store is readable by every user
# on the machine.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=000000000000-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The origin this instance is reached at. Read by adapter-node itself, and not
# optional for any instance served over plain http.
#
# Without it adapter-node derives the origin from the request, and its default
# protocol is `https` — it never looks at the socket. So an instance on
# http://192.168.1.50:3000 believes it is https, and two things break at once,
# both of them quietly:
#
#   * the session cookie goes out with `Secure`, which a browser silently drops
#     over http. Sign-in answers 204 and the app behaves as though it never
#     happened.
#   * the Google redirect URI is built as https://…, which matches no console
#     entry, so sign-in fails on Google's page as `redirect_uri_mismatch`.
#
# Behind a TLS-terminating proxy, PROTOCOL_HEADER below does the same job and
# this can stay unset.
# ORIGIN=http://192.168.1.50:3000

# Extra origins allowed to call /api/* from a browser, comma-separated. The
# Capacitor origins are always allowed and the web surface is same-origin, so
# most instances never need this. Each entry is normalised to scheme://host:port,
# so a trailing slash is forgiven; anything that is not a URL is dropped with a
# warning on startup rather than silently matching nothing.
# CORS_ORIGINS=https://kilorep.example.com

# Behind a reverse proxy (Caddy, nginx, Traefik) these are not optional — they
# are read by adapter-node itself, and without them the server draws the wrong
# conclusion from every request rather than failing visibly:
#
#   ADDRESS_HEADER    which header carries the real client address. Unset, every
#                     request appears to come from the proxy, and the login
#                     throttle counts the whole instance as one caller — ten
#                     wrong passwords from one attacker then lock out every user
#                     for fifteen minutes, with no way to clear it early.
#   XFF_DEPTH         how many proxies you run. A client can prepend entries to
#                     x-forwarded-for, so only the last XFF_DEPTH are yours to
#                     believe; the default of 1 is right for a single proxy.
#   PROTOCOL_HEADER   without it the server sees http and leaves the session
#                     cookie un-Secure on a connection that deserved the flag.
#
# ADDRESS_HEADER=x-forwarded-for
# XFF_DEPTH=1
# PROTOCOL_HEADER=x-forwarded-proto
